    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nAntilia Is a Sculptural Yacht Concept With Generous Open-Air Areas and Three Jacuzzis\n18 photos\nPhoto: Baran Akalin Design\nFor visionary sea lovers looking to invest in an avant-garde superyacht, the best designs are those that are ready to transition from aspirational blueprint to reality. Turkish shipyard KRC Yachting has just unveiled one such design in the form of the Antilia superyacht concept.\nDeveloped in collaboration with Baran Akalın Design, this ready-to-build concept blends an architectural exterior with spacious living areas and a restrained approach to luxury.\nMeasuring 43 meters (141 feet) long, Antilia is designed to offer comfort and flexibility for extended stays at sea. It boasts a massive beach club with transforming balconies at the water’s edge, three separate Jacuzzis, and a huge upper-deck owner’s cabin. According to the designers, the concept is ready for production, with construction estimated to take 24 months.\nThe vessel’s imposing profile is mainly due to a sculpted hull with clean architectural lines and impressive yet carefully-proportioned volumes. The designers intentionally avoided visual complexity or excessive ornamentation, relying on bold shapes, like angular geometric chamfers rather than traditional soft curves or 90-degree edges. Expansive, heavily tinted bands of glass wrap around the hull and superstructure, and a dramatic forward-sweeping line bridges the main deck and upper decks, creating a strong sense of motion even when the yacht is at anchor.\nPhoto: Baran Akalin Design Like most modern concepts, the Antilia is designed around life at sea, so open air areas abound on board. Large, strategically positioned openings allow for a seamless indoor-outdoor flow, blurring the lines between interior living areas and al-fresco spaces.\nThe generous beach club perfectly exemplifies this seamless flow. Here, opening balconies extend the beach club into a water-edge living space that blends indoor comfort with direct sea views. From the swimming platform, a pair of illuminated stairs lead to a raised seating area that provides a comfortable place to relax while enjoying the surrounding vistas. The beach club also features one of the yacht’s three Jacuzzis, with the other two positioned on the upper deck and flybridge.\nSpeaking of the flybridge, this elevated area comprises a bar for relaxed drinks, a shaded lounge, and sunbathing space aft. Guests have other lounging options as well. One of them is located aft of the main saloon, allowing guests to easily move between indoor and outdoor spaces.\nPhoto: Baran Akalin Design With a beam of 8.55 meters (28 feet) and a draft of 2.4 meters (8 feet), Antilia 43 superyacht has a gross tonnage of 498 GT. In terms of interior layout, the designers proposed an eight-stateroom arrangement, with space for up to twelve guests. For the owner, they envisioned a luxurious suite on the upper deck, complete with a private balcony on the upper terrace. The suite can also be accessed via a staircase from the main deck.\nAs for the convivial areas, the spacious salon on the main deck includes a lounge with plush seating and a dining area, and it seamlessly connects with the outdoor seating areas as well. Antilia’s interior spaces will be finished with natural textures and materials to create a warm, inviting ambiance.\nPhoto: Baran Akalin Design Both the choice of materials and spatial configuration are meant to contribute to visual continuity and a sense of calm and serenity. Of course, prospective owners are free to personalize the interior configuration, furnishings, finishes, and decorative details to suit their preferences.\nThe yacht concept also features a tender garage and a gym, both of them located beneath the beach club and both customizable to the owner’s requirements.\nIn terms of propulsion, the Antilia motor yacht will be powered by two MAN D2868 LE436 engines, each producing 1,200 horsepower. The yacht is expected to reach a top speed of 14 knots and cruise at 12 knots.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nyacht concept Antilia superyacht KRC Yachting Baran Akaln Design yacht design\nAbout the author: Ancuta Iosub\nAfter spending a few years as a copy editor, Ancuta decided to put down the eraser and pick up the writer's pencil. Her favorites subjects are unusual car designs, travel trailers and everything related to the great outdoors. Full profile\nUP NEXT Chevrolet Corvette Z06X Would Cost ZR1 Money, Says Corvette Chief Engineer Tony Roma",
